const e=[{id:"sv-1-741",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The telecom regulator requires that **emergency calls are connected within 10 seconds**. A Black Belt records this as a specification limit for the call-routing process. Which source of specifications does this limit come from?",options:["Business","Compliance","Customer","Design"],answer:1,hint:"Specifications come from four sources: Customer, Business, Compliance and Design. Ask who imposes the requirement.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book names four sources of specifications: **Customer** (VOC), **Business** (internal policy and economics), **Compliance** (laws and regulators) and **Design** (engineering or technical limits)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The 10-second limit is imposed by the **regulator**, so it is a legal or regulatory requirement — a compliance specification."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A customer may also want fast connection, but the limit as stated is set by the regulator, not by VOC, company policy or an engineering drawing."}],final:"Regulator-imposed limit = Compliance specification → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — business specifications come from internal policy or economics, such as cost or margin rules.","","Incorrect — customer specifications come from the voice of the customer, not from a regulator.","Incorrect — design specifications come from engineering or technical limits, such as drawing tolerances."],trap:"Ask WHO sets the limit. A regulator or law = Compliance, even if customers would like the same thing.",bookRef:"Book p. 212"},{id:"sv-1-742",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which description fits a **defective** unit?",options:["A single failure to meet one specification","A characteristic that can be judged acceptable or unacceptable","A unit that has been corrected after a failed check","A unit that has one or more defects"],answer:3,hint:"Defect = one failure against one specification. Defective = the whole unit that carries at least one defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 585) separates a **defect** (any result outside a specification) from a **defective** (a unit with one or more defects)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stem asks about the **unit**, so the answer is the unit carrying at least one defect."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A single failure is a defect; a characteristic that can pass or fail is an opportunity; correcting a unit is rework."}],final:"Defective = unit with one or more defects → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — one failure against one specification is a defect, not a defective unit.","Incorrect — a characteristic that can be acceptable or unacceptable is an opportunity.","Incorrect — correcting a failed unit describes rework, not the definition of a defective.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 585, 587"},{id:"sv-1-743",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The [?] is the value the customer would like all output of the process to be centred on.`,options:["limit","mean","target","tolerance"],answer:2,hint:"One value is preferred by the customer; the limits are only the edges of acceptable.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 112) says the **target** is the value the customer prefers outputs to be centred on; if none is given, use what the average should be."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The sentence describes a preferred centre value, not an edge of acceptability."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A limit marks the edge of acceptable; the mean is what the process actually delivers; tolerance is the allowed spread between limits."}],final:"Preferred centre value = target → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a specification limit marks the edge of acceptable performance, not the preferred centre.","Incorrect — the mean is what the process actually produces, which may differ from what the customer wants.","","Incorrect — tolerance is the allowed range between the limits, not a single preferred value."],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-1-744",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"A circuit board is inspected against five CTQs and fails three of them. This board counts as **three defectives**. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Count defects per failure; count defectives per unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each failed CTQ is one **defect**. A **defective** is a unit with one or more defects (book p. 585)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The board has **three defects** but is **one defective** unit."}],final:"Three defects on one board = one defective → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this mixes the two terms; three failed CTQs are three defects on a single defective unit.",""],trap:"Defects can be more than one per unit; defectives can never be more than the number of units.",bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-745",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which element of a **CTQ tree** is written in the customer’s own, often vague, words, such as “I want a bill I can understand”?",options:["The driver","The CTQ","The specification limit","The need"],answer:3,hint:"A CTQ tree reads left to right: need → drivers → CTQs, from vague to measurable.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ tree starts with the **need** in VOC language, breaks it into **drivers** (what the need means) and ends with measurable **CTQs** that carry limits."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“I want a bill I can understand” is the customer’s own wording, so it sits at the need level."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Drivers are the team’s interpretation; CTQs and their limits are measurable statements, not customer wording."}],final:"Customer wording = the need (left of the tree) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a driver interprets the need (for example “clear charges”), one level more specific.","Incorrect — a CTQ is the measurable end of the tree, with a metric and a limit.","Incorrect — a specification limit is part of a measurable CTQ, not customer wording.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 509"},{id:"sv-1-746",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A cable plant’s customer says the cable “must fit our ducts”. The team writes the CTQ: **outer diameter 12.0 mm, LSL 11.8 mm, USL 12.2 mm**. What does the value 12.0 mm represent?",options:["The lower specification limit","The tolerance","The process mean","The target"],answer:3,hint:"Parts of a complete CTQ: characteristic, metric, target, specification limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A complete CTQ has a characteristic, a metric/unit, a **target** and specification limits (book p. 112)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"11.8 and 12.2 are named as LSL and USL; 12.0 sits between them as the value the customer wants output centred on — the target."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The LSL is 11.8; tolerance is the allowed range between the limits; the process mean is measured from data, not written into the CTQ."}],final:"12.0 mm is the preferred centre value = target → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the LSL in this CTQ is 11.8 mm, the lower edge of acceptable.","Incorrect — tolerance is the allowed range between LSL and USL, not a single value.","Incorrect — the process mean comes from measured data; a CTQ states what the customer wants, not what the process does.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 112, 589"},{id:"sv-1-747",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **target** of a CTQ is CORRECT?",options:["When the customer gives no target, the team uses the value the average should be","The target is always set equal to the upper specification limit","A target is only needed when the specification is two-sided","The target is taken from the current process mean in the baseline"],answer:0,hint:"Target = what the customer would like output centred on; the book says what to do when none is given.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 112) says the target is the value the customer would like all output centred on; if the customer gives none, use what the **average should be**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the statement that matches this fallback rule is correct."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A target is not a limit, is useful for one-sided CTQs too, and comes from customer needs rather than current performance."}],final:"No customer target → use what the average should be → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the USL is the edge of acceptable; the target is the preferred centre, inside the limits.","Incorrect — one-sided CTQs can also have a target, such as a preferred response time below the USL.","Incorrect — the baseline mean describes current performance; the target reflects what the customer wants."],bookRef:"Book p. 112"},{id:"sv-1-748",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about **CTQs, KPIs and SLAs** are CORRECT?

1. A CTQ defines what “good” means for one unit of output.
2. A KPI summarises CTQ performance across many units over a period.
3. Meeting the SLA proves that every CTQ is met.
4. An SLA should always be written before the CTQs are defined.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Healthy chain: VOC → CTQ → KPI → (sometimes) SLA.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ judges each unit; a KPI aggregates CTQ results over time; an SLA turns a KPI into a contractual promise."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 1 and 2 match the chain. Statement 3 is false — a vendor can meet an SLA on an automatic e-mail while customers still wait. Statement 4 reverses the chain."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 are correct → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: SLA compliance does not prove the customer’s CTQs are met.","Incorrect — statements 3 and 4 are both false: the SLA comes after the CTQ and does not prove it.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: the SLA follows VOC and CTQs, it does not come first."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.2"},{id:"sv-1-749",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the four sources of specifications described for CTQs?",options:["Benchmark","Business","Compliance","Design"],answer:0,hint:"Recall the four sources: Customer, Business, Compliance, Design.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The four sources of specifications are **Customer, Business, Compliance and Design**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Business, Compliance and Design are on the list; Benchmark is not."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A benchmark compares performance with others; it can inspire a goal but is not one of the four specification sources."}],final:"Benchmark is not a specification source → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — business requirements, such as cost policies, are one of the four sources.","Incorrect — compliance (laws and regulators) is one of the four sources.","Incorrect — design (engineering and technical limits) is one of the four sources."],bookRef:"Book p. 212"},{id:"sv-1-750",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A customer of a printed-circuit-board maker says: *“Your boards must be reliable.”* Which is the BEST CTQ to derive from this need?",options:["Field failures within 12 months of shipment ≤ 0.5% of boards, from warranty records","Boards are built to a high standard of reliability at all times","Improve board reliability by 20% before the end of the year","Install automated optical inspection on assembly line 2"],answer:0,hint:"A good CTQ is measurable, has a defined measurement method and a limit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ turns a vague need into a **measurable characteristic with a limit** and a clear data source."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the field-failure option names a metric (field failures in 12 months), a limit (≤ 0.5%) and how it is measured (warranty records)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Restating the need is still vague; a percentage improvement is a project goal; installing equipment is a solution."}],final:"Measurable characteristic + limit + data source = CTQ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this repeats the customer’s vague words; it has no metric or limit, so no defect can be counted.","Incorrect — this is a project goal, not a CTQ; it has no unit-level limit or defect rule.","Incorrect — this is a solution, which jumps ahead of the need and says nothing about the customer requirement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 112, 585"},{id:"sv-1-751",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Two hundred handsets are each checked against **three CTQs**. Minitab tallies how many defects each handset has:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Defects per handset

Defects  Count  CumCnt
      0    170     170
      1     22     192
      2      6     198
      3      2     200
      N=   200
\`\`\`

Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["Only 2 handsets are defective","The Count column shows the number of defects in each row","A handset with two defects counts as two defectives","170 handsets have no defect against any of the three CTQs"],answer:3,hint:"The Count column counts units (handsets); defects are failures per unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defective** is a unit with one or more defects. In this tally, each row groups handsets by how many defects they have."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The row Defects = 0 shows Count = 170, so 170 handsets passed all three CTQs."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Every handset in rows 1, 2 and 3 is defective, not only the last row; Count counts handsets; two defects still make one defective."}],final:"170 handsets are defect-free → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 2 is only the handsets with three defects; every handset with one or more defects is defective.","Incorrect — Count is the number of handsets in each row, not the number of defects.","Incorrect — a unit with any number of defects is one defective; the defects are counted separately.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-752",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"The CTQ for enterprise faults is **“service restored within 4 hours”**. Which statement about this CTQ is CORRECT?",options:["It needs a lower limit of 0 hours before it can be used","It is two-sided because restoration can be early or late","Its target must be set equal to 4 hours","It has an upper limit only, so a very fast restoration is never a defect"],answer:3,hint:"Ask whether results can be too low as well as too high.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Specification limits can be **one-sided** (only a USL or only an LSL) or **two-sided** (book p. 589)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Nobody complains that a fault was fixed too quickly, so this CTQ has only a USL of 4 hours; any result above 4 hours is a defect."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An LSL is not needed, the CTQ is not two-sided, and 4 hours is the edge of acceptable, not the preferred value."}],final:"Restore time → USL only (one-sided) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a one-sided CTQ is valid without an LSL; zero hours is a natural boundary, not a specification.","Incorrect — two-sided limits apply when too early is also unacceptable, such as a timed callback window.","Incorrect — 4 hours is the USL, the edge of acceptable; the preferred value would be lower.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-753",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is an example of a specification that comes from the **Design** source?",options:["A connector pin diameter of 0.50 ± 0.02 mm set on the engineering drawing so mating parts fit","A policy that the cost per contact in the call centre must not exceed OMR 1.20","A regulator’s limit on the radio power a base station may emit","A customer’s request that installers arrive within the booked two-hour slot"],answer:0,hint:"Four sources: Customer, Business, Compliance, Design. Design = technical/engineering limits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Design specifications come from **engineering or technical requirements**, such as drawing tolerances needed for parts to work together."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The pin tolerance is set on the engineering drawing so that parts fit — a design specification."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The cost cap is a business rule, the radio limit is compliance, and the arrival window is a customer requirement."}],final:"Drawing tolerance = Design specification → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an internal cost limit is a business specification.","Incorrect — a limit imposed by a regulator is a compliance specification.","Incorrect — an arrival window the customer asks for is a customer specification."],bookRef:"Book p. 212"},{id:"sv-1-754",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"An inspector checks a sample of units against several CTQs. Which statement comparing the number of **defects** and **defectives** in the sample is CORRECT?",options:["The number of defects can be equal to or greater than the number of defectives","The number of defectives is always greater than the number of defects","The number of defects and defectives is always the same","The number of defectives can be greater than the number of units inspected"],answer:0,hint:"One unit can carry several defects but counts as only one defective.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defect** is one failure against a specification; a **defective** is a unit with one or more defects (book p. 585)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Each defective unit has at least one defect and may have several, so defects ≥ defectives."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Defectives cannot exceed defects, the two counts are only equal when no unit has more than one defect, and defectives cannot exceed the units inspected."}],final:"Defects ≥ defectives → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — every defective unit has at least one defect, so defectives can never exceed defects.","Incorrect — the counts are equal only when no unit has more than one defect.","Incorrect — each unit can be defective at most once, so defectives cannot exceed the units inspected."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-755",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt reports “**46 defects** found in 400 business orders”. The Champion asks: “How many **customers** received a faulty order?” Each order goes to one customer. What should the Black Belt report?",options:["The average number of defects per order","The number of orders with one or more defects","The total number of defects again, because it answers the question","The number of CTQs checked on each order"],answer:1,hint:"Customers feel defective units; the defect count can include several failures on one order.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defective** is a unit with one or more defects. One order may carry several defects but reaches only one customer."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The Champion asks how many customers were affected, so the count needed is defective orders."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The defect total over-counts orders with several defects; the number of CTQs is an opportunity count; an average per order does not count customers."}],final:"Customers affected = defective orders (units with ≥ 1 defect) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an average per order describes defect density, not how many customers got a faulty order.","","Incorrect — 46 defects may sit on fewer orders, so the defect total over-states the customers affected.","Incorrect — the CTQs checked per order are opportunities, not a count of faulty orders."],trap:"Defects count failures; defectives count units (here, customers).",bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-756",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Under the common convention used on the exam, which cost categories make up the **Cost of Poor Quality (COPQ)**?",options:["Appraisal and external failure costs","Internal failure and external failure costs","Prevention and appraisal costs","Prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure costs"],answer:1,hint:"COPQ is the failure part of the cost of quality.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common convention: **COPQ = internal failure + external failure**. Prevention and appraisal are the cost of good quality."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stem asks for COPQ, so only the two failure categories belong."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Prevention + appraisal is the cost of conformance; all four together is total COQ; mixing appraisal with external failure follows no convention."}],final:"COPQ = internal failure + external failure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this pairing leaves out internal failure and adds appraisal, which is not a failure cost.","","Incorrect — prevention and appraisal together are the cost of good quality (cost of conformance).","Incorrect — the sum of all four categories is the total Cost of Quality (COQ), not COPQ."],trap:"Summing all four categories gives COQ — the most tempting wrong answer.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-757",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the **Cost of Quality (COQ)**?",options:["The cost of training staff and error-proofing processes","The total of prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure costs","The cost of defects found by the customer only","The cost of inspecting and testing products before shipment"],answer:1,hint:"COQ is the total; COPQ is the failure part.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**COQ** is the total cost linked to quality across all four categories: P + A + IF + EF."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stem asks for the total, so all four categories are included."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Customer-found defects are only external failure; inspection is appraisal; training and error-proofing are prevention."}],final:"COQ = P + A + IF + EF → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — training and error-proofing are prevention costs, one of the four categories.","","Incorrect — defects found by the customer are only the external failure category.","Incorrect — inspection and testing are appraisal costs, one of the four categories."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-758",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Why does a Black Belt estimate the COPQ of a problem in the **Define** phase?",options:["To prove which input variable is the root cause of the defects","To set the control limits for monitoring the improved process","To decide how many appraisers are needed for the Gage R&R study","To show whether the problem is worth solving and support the business case"],answer:3,hint:"Define asks: is this problem worth a project?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A COPQ estimate in Define puts a money value on the problem, showing **whether it is worth solving** and feeding the business case."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“Estimate COPQ” is a Define step on the DMAIC roadmap (book p. 230)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Root-cause proof is Analyze, control limits are Control, and Gage R&R planning is Measure."}],final:"COPQ in Define = justify the project / business case → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — proving root causes belongs to the Analyze phase, not to a cost estimate.","Incorrect — control limits are calculated from process data in the Control phase.","Incorrect — Gage R&R planning is a Measure-phase activity unrelated to COPQ.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-759",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does the **1–10–100 rule** of thumb illustrate?",options:["A defect costs far more the later it is found, especially once it reaches the customer","One prevention activity removes ten appraisal steps and a hundred defects","Total quality cost should be split 1% prevention, 10% appraisal and 100% failure","A process needs one inspector for every ten operators and a hundred units"],answer:0,hint:"Prevent → fix inside → suffer at the customer: each stage multiplies the cost.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The rule says a defect costing 1 to prevent may cost 10 to fix internally and 100 once it reaches the customer. The ratios are illustrative; the **direction** is reliable."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The point is that moving effort upstream is cheaper."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other options invent ratios between activities, budgets or staffing that the rule does not describe."}],final:"Later detection = much higher cost → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the rule compares cost per defect at different stages, not counts of activities removed.","Incorrect — the rule is not a budget split; these figures do not even form a valid split.","Incorrect — the rule says nothing about inspector staffing ratios."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-760",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"As a quality programme matures, which pattern of quality costs is typically seen?",options:["Prevention rises a little, failure costs fall a lot and total COQ falls","Prevention falls, appraisal rises and failure costs stay the same","All four categories rise together as more is measured","Failure costs rise because more defects are now detected"],answer:0,hint:"Spend a little upstream, save a lot downstream.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Investing in **prevention** removes causes, so appraisal can later fall and **failure costs fall sharply**, lowering total COQ."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only “prevention up a little, failure down a lot” shows that upstream shift and its result."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Cutting prevention, rising totals or rising failure costs describe a programme that is not working."}],final:"Prevention ↑ a little, failure ↓ a lot, total ↓ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — cutting prevention while adding appraisal finds defects but leaves their causes in place.","Incorrect — a maturing programme lowers total COQ; it does not raise every category.","Incorrect — better prevention reduces the defects made, so failure costs fall, not rise."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-761",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"What decides whether a failure cost is classed as **internal** or **external**?",options:["How much the repair cost","Who found the defect, or whom it affected","Where the defect was repaired","Which department caused the defect"],answer:1,hint:"Before the customer = internal; at or after the customer = external.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The test is **who found it, or whom it affected** — not where it was fixed."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A wrong speed profile caught by a pre-activation check is internal; the same error found by the customer is external."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Repair location, the department at fault and the size of the bill do not change the category."}],final:"Classification = who found it / whom it affected → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both internal and external failures can be cheap or expensive.","","Incorrect — a customer-found defect fixed in your own workshop is still external failure.","Incorrect — the cause department matters for root cause, not for the cost category."],trap:"“Fixed in our own workshop” does not make a customer-found defect internal.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-762",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Who should a Black Belt agree the **COPQ estimation method and figures** with before they go into the business case?",options:["The customer","The Master Black Belt only","Finance","The process operators"],answer:2,hint:"Money figures need a money owner.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"COPQ assumptions — especially for hidden and hard-to-measure costs — should be **agreed with Finance**, who validate project benefits."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The business case uses money figures, so Finance makes them credible."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Operators supply data, the customer supplies VOC, and a Master Black Belt coaches method — none of them owns financial validation."}],final:"Finance validates COPQ figures → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customers provide VOC and requirements, not the organisation’s cost figures.","Incorrect — a Master Black Belt coaches the method but does not replace Finance sign-off.","","Incorrect — operators can supply data on rework, but they do not validate financial figures."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-763",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why are external failure costs usually **higher** than internal failure costs for the same defect?",options:["They are recorded at list price instead of at internal cost","They always involve scrapping the whole product","They are counted twice, once by Operations and once by Finance","They add customer handling, penalties and the risk of losing the customer"],answer:3,hint:"Think of what happens after the customer meets the defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Once a defect reaches the customer, the fix is joined by **complaint handling, credits or penalties, repeat visits and churn risk**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Those extra consequences make external failure the most expensive stage."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other options describe accounting quirks or scrapping, which are not why external failure costs more."}],final:"External = fix + customer handling + penalties + churn risk → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the extra cost is real consequence, not a pricing convention.","Incorrect — many external failures are repaired, not scrapped; scrap is often internal failure.","Incorrect — double-counting is an error to avoid, not a reason external failure costs more.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-764",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Six Sigma improves outputs by finding and fixing the X’s in **Y = f(X)**. In cost-of-quality terms, what kind of strategy is this?",options:["A prevention strategy","An appraisal strategy","An internal failure strategy","An external failure strategy"],answer:0,hint:"Fixing causes stops defects being made.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Fixing the X’s removes the causes of defects, so defects are **not made** — that is **prevention**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The strategy acts before defects occur, not by finding or repairing them."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Appraisal finds defects after they are made; the two failure categories are costs of defects, not strategies."}],final:"Fix the X’s = prevention → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — appraisal detects defects by checking; it does not change the causes.","Incorrect — internal failure is the cost of defects found before delivery, not a strategy.","Incorrect — external failure is the cost of defects that reach the customer."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-765",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A customer is billed twice for the same month and the operator refunds the overcharge after the customer complains. In which cost-of-quality category is the refund?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:1,hint:"Who found the defect — the company or the customer?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defects found by, or affecting, the **customer** create external failure costs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The customer found the double billing and complained, so the refund is external failure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No checking, internal catch or upstream prevention took place."}],final:"Customer-found billing error → External failure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — appraisal costs are spent checking or inspecting to find defects.","","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.","Incorrect — prevention costs are spent to stop defects from happening in the first place."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-766",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A product team holds **quality-planning workshops** before launching a new broadband router to agree standards and error-proof the set-up steps. In which cost-of-quality category are these workshops?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:3,hint:"Is the money spent before, during or after a defect exists?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention costs are spent to **stop defects happening** — planning, training, error-proofing, design reviews."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The workshops happen before launch, to stop set-up errors from occurring."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing is being inspected or repaired, and no customer has been affected."}],final:"Quality planning before launch → Prevention → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — appraisal costs are spent checking or inspecting to find defects.","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-767",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The billing system rejects 300 orders a month because of data errors, and back-office staff **re-key them before any invoice is issued**. In which category is the re-keying cost?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:2,hint:"Found before or after the customer?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Internal failure = defects found **before** the customer is affected — rework, re-keying, scrap, overtime."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The errors are caught and corrected before invoices go out, so it is internal failure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"It is correction of a defect, not checking (appraisal) or stopping it (prevention), and the customer is not affected."}],final:"Re-keying before invoicing → Internal failure → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the rejection check is appraisal, but the re-keying that corrects the errors is failure cost.","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","","Incorrect — prevention costs are spent to stop defects from happening in the first place."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-768",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A machine shop measures the **first part of every new batch** against the drawing before releasing the batch to run. In which category is this inspection?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:0,hint:"Checking to find defects is not the same as fixing them.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Appraisal costs are spent **checking or inspecting** to find defects — inspection, testing, audits, calibration."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"First-article inspection checks whether the set-up is producing good parts; it finds problems, it does not correct them."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No defect is being repaired and no customer has been affected; it also does not change the cause."}],final:"First-article inspection → Appraisal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.","Incorrect — inspection finds defects after set-up; prevention would change the process so the error cannot occur."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-769",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the **hidden factory** make COPQ look smaller than it really is?",options:["The hidden factory consists only of appraisal costs, which COPQ excludes","The corrective work is paid through normal salaries and overtime, so it has no separate cost line","Hidden-factory costs are always removed from the accounts by Finance","Hidden-factory work only happens in manufacturing, not in services"],answer:1,hint:"No invoice, no accounting line — the work looks like ordinary work.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 586) defines the hidden factory as corrective, non-value-added work that is **not recognised** as waste."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Re-visits, data corrections and chasing are paid in ordinary wages, so they never appear as a COPQ line."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Finance does not remove them, services have a large hidden factory, and the work is failure, not appraisal."}],final:"Hidden factory = unrecognised corrective work paid as normal work → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — hidden-factory work is corrective (failure) work, not checking.","","Incorrect — Finance does not delete these costs; they were never separated in the first place.","Incorrect — services have a large hidden factory: re-visits, duplicate tickets and manual workarounds."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-770",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"When a team maps the **actual** process (not the official one), what does the book say they almost always find?",options:["The actual process has no rework once it is documented","The actual process costs the same as the budget shows","The actual process is bigger and more costly than people think, with hidden operations","The actual process is simpler than the documented procedure"],answer:2,hint:"Mapping reveals the hidden factory.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 100) says the actual process is almost always **bigger and more costly** than people think, full of “hidden operations”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Those hidden operations — rework loops, checks, workarounds — are part of COPQ that the budget does not show."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other options assume the official picture is complete, which mapping usually disproves."}],final:"Actual process = bigger, more costly, hidden operations → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — documenting a process does not remove rework; mapping exposes it.","Incorrect — hidden operations are paid through normal budgets, so the real cost is higher than it appears.","","Incorrect — mapping usually reveals extra loops and steps, so the real process is more complex."],bookRef:"Book p. 100"},{id:"sv-1-771",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which spending is MOST likely to lower the **total** cost of quality over time?",options:["Adding a second inspection team at the end of the order process","Setting up a faster complaint-handling desk for billing errors","Increasing the reserve held for warranty claims","Error-proofing the order-entry form so invalid data cannot be submitted"],answer:3,hint:"Prevent → detect → fix → suffer. The earliest stage is the cheapest.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention removes the cause, so fewer defects are made and failure costs fall — total COQ falls."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Error-proofing the form stops invalid orders at source: a prevention cost."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"More inspection is appraisal, faster complaint handling and a bigger warranty reserve deal with failures already made."}],final:"Prevention (error-proofing) lowers total COQ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — extra end inspection raises appraisal cost but leaves the cause in place.","Incorrect — faster complaint handling manages external failures; it does not stop them.","Incorrect — a warranty reserve only funds external failures; it does not reduce them.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-772",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Internal failure costs such as rework and scrap are the money value of which **Lean waste**?",options:["Defects","Motion","Inventory","Waiting"],answer:0,hint:"Rework and scrap exist because something was not right first time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Rework is the activity to correct defects (book p. 588); scrap is defective output thrown away. Both are the cost of the **Defects** waste."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Internal failure costs are created by defective work found before the customer."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Motion, inventory and waiting are wastes too, but they are not the direct source of rework and scrap."}],final:"Rework and scrap = cost of the Defects waste → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — motion is unnecessary movement of people, not correction of faulty work.","Incorrect — inventory is excess material or work waiting, not the correction of defects.","Incorrect — waiting is idle time between steps, not rework or scrap."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-773",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **internal failure costs** is CORRECT?",options:["They are always smaller than appraisal costs","They arise from defects found before the product or service reaches the customer","They include warranty claims and refunds paid to customers","They are spent to stop defects from occurring in the first place"],answer:1,hint:"Internal = caught inside, before the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Internal failure costs come from defects **found before the customer** is affected: rework, scrap, re-keying, overtime."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the statement about defects found before the customer matches that definition."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Warranty and refunds are external failure; stopping defects is prevention; no fixed size order exists between categories."}],final:"Internal failure = defects found before the customer → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — there is no fixed rule; in a weak process internal failure is often far larger than appraisal.","","Incorrect — warranty claims and refunds come from defects that reached the customer: external failure.","Incorrect — spending to stop defects occurring describes prevention costs."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-774",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"COPQ is often drawn as an **iceberg**. Which statement about the iceberg is CORRECT?",options:["Costs below the waterline each have their own accounting line","Only external failure costs appear in the iceberg","The costs below the waterline are usually much larger than those above it","The costs above the waterline are the hidden factory"],answer:2,hint:"Above water = visible, costed lines; below = unrecognised costs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Above the water sit visible costs with their own lines — scrap, warranty, SLA credits. Below sits a **much bigger** mass that nobody costs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement that the hidden part is larger matches the iceberg picture."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The hidden factory is below the water, hidden costs have no accounting line, and both internal and external failures appear."}],final:"Below the waterline = larger, hidden costs → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — hidden costs are hidden precisely because they have no separate accounting line.","Incorrect — the iceberg holds internal failures too, such as rework and re-keying.","","Incorrect — the hidden factory sits below the waterline, because it is not recognised."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-775",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about **appraisal costs** is CORRECT?",options:["Appraisal costs are part of COPQ under the common convention","Raising appraisal spending is the best way to reduce failure costs","Appraisal costs arise only after the customer has been affected","Appraisal finds defects but does not remove their cause"],answer:3,hint:"Checking detects; it does not prevent.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Appraisal is spending to **find** defects — inspection, testing, audits. The defects are still made."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So more inspection may catch more defects but leaves the cause in place."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Under the common convention appraisal is cost of good quality, not COPQ; prevention, not appraisal, is the best lever; appraisal happens before delivery."}],final:"Appraisal detects defects; it does not remove causes → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — under the common convention COPQ is only internal and external failure; appraisal is cost of good quality.","Incorrect — prevention is the best lever; more appraisal mainly moves failures from external to internal.","Incorrect — appraisal is checking inside the company; customer-affected costs are external failure.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-776",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Some authors count appraisal costs inside COPQ, while others count only failure costs. Which statement about this difference is CORRECT?",options:["The business case should state which convention it uses for COPQ","Appraisal must always be included, or the COPQ estimate is invalid","The difference does not matter, because appraisal costs are always small","The convention changes only whether external failure is included"],answer:0,hint:"Two conventions exist; transparency is what matters.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The common convention is **COPQ = failure costs only**; some sources add appraisal."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Because both exist, a Black Belt should **state which convention** the business case uses (and use failure-only on the exam unless told otherwise)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Neither convention is “invalid”, appraisal can be large, and external failure is in COPQ under both conventions."}],final:"State the convention used in the business case → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the common convention excludes appraisal; neither convention makes the estimate invalid.","Incorrect — appraisal costs can be large, such as big inspection teams, so the choice changes the figure.","Incorrect — external failure is in COPQ under both conventions; the difference concerns appraisal."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-777",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the COPQ estimate made in **Define** is CORRECT?",options:["It is only needed when the project has hard savings","It is a first estimate that is refined as more is learned in Measure","It is a final figure that may not be changed after the charter is signed","It must be based on a completed root-cause analysis"],answer:1,hint:"The Measure checklist asks: “Have you identified more COPQ?”",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"“Estimate COPQ” is a Define step (book p. 230); the Measure checklist then asks “Have you identified more COPQ?” (p. 231)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So the Define figure is a **first estimate**, refined as data and process knowledge grow."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"It is not frozen, does not wait for Analyze, and supports the business case whatever kind of savings is expected."}],final:"Define COPQ = first estimate, refined in Measure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — COPQ helps show whether any problem is worth solving, not only problems with hard savings.","","Incorrect — the Measure checklist expects the estimate to grow as more COPQ is found.","Incorrect — root-cause analysis happens in Analyze; the Define estimate comes before it."],bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231"},{id:"sv-1-778",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:1,stem:`Which statements about what COPQ includes (common convention) are CORRECT?

1. COPQ includes the cost of training staff.
2. COPQ includes scrap and rework.
3. COPQ includes warranty claims.
4. COPQ includes calibrating test equipment.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"COPQ = internal + external failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Under the common convention COPQ contains only failure costs: internal (scrap, rework) and external (warranty, refunds)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statement 2 is internal failure and statement 3 is external failure. Training (1) is prevention; calibration (4) is appraisal."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: training is a prevention cost, not part of COPQ.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: calibration is an appraisal cost, not a failure cost.","Incorrect — statements 1 and 4 are both false: they are prevention and appraisal costs."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-779",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:1,stem:`Which costs are **prevention** costs?

1. Re-testing units that failed final test.
2. Handling customer complaints about late delivery.
3. Training engineers on a new installation procedure.
4. Error-proofing the order form so required fields cannot be left blank.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Prevention = money spent so defects do not happen.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention costs are spent before defects exist: training, planning, error-proofing, design reviews."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 3 and 4 stop errors at source. Re-testing failed units (1) is internal failure; complaint handling (2) is external failure."}],final:"Statements 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both statements 1 and 2 are failure costs: re-testing is internal, complaints are external.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: complaint handling is an external failure cost.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: re-testing failed units is an internal failure cost."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-780",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the **hidden factory** are CORRECT?

1. It consists of corrective work that is not recognised as waste.
2. It always appears as a separate line in the profit-and-loss report.
3. It is made up mainly of warranty and refund payments.
4. It is usually paid for through normal salaries and overtime.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Hidden factory (book p. 586) = unrecognised corrective, non-value-added work.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The hidden factory is corrective work nobody recognises as waste; it is hidden because it has no invoice."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 1 and 4 fit. Statement 2 contradicts “hidden”; statement 3 describes visible external failure costs."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: hidden-factory work has no separate accounting line.","Incorrect — statements 2 and 3 are false: the work is unrecorded and is not warranty or refunds.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: warranty and refunds are visible external failure costs.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-781",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about **investing in prevention** are CORRECT?

1. Moving quality effort upstream is usually cheaper than fixing defects later.
2. As a quality programme matures, failure costs typically fall.
3. Adding more final inspection removes the root cause of defects.
4. Total cost of quality always rises when prevention spending rises.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Prevent → detect → fix → suffer: earlier is cheaper.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention removes causes, so failure costs fall and total COQ falls, even if prevention spending rises a little."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 1 and 2 match. Statement 3 confuses appraisal with prevention; statement 4 reverses the usual result."}],final:"Statements 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: final inspection detects defects but leaves their cause in place.","Incorrect — statements 3 and 4 are both false: inspection is not prevention, and total COQ usually falls.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: well-aimed prevention usually lowers the total."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-782",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which are **external failure** costs?

1. Re-keying orders rejected by the billing system before invoicing.
2. Refunds to customers who were billed twice.
3. Replacing faulty modems under warranty.
4. Calibrating the line-test equipment.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"External = defect found by, or affecting, the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"External failure costs arise once the customer has met the defect: refunds, warranty, complaints, credits."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 2 and 3 reached the customer. Re-keying before invoicing (1) is internal failure; calibration (4) is appraisal."}],final:"Statements 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: the errors were fixed before invoicing, so it is internal failure.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: calibration is an appraisal cost.","Incorrect — statements 1 and 4 are internal failure and appraisal, not external failure."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-783",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] costs are incurred to find defects by checking, testing or inspecting work.`,options:["Appraisal","Failure","Prevention","Rework"],answer:0,hint:"Detecting is different from preventing or fixing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Appraisal = spending to **find** defects by checking: QA audits, test calls, inspection, calibration."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The sentence describes finding defects by checking, so the word is appraisal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Prevention stops defects; failure and rework are what happens after a defect exists."}],final:"Checking to find defects = appraisal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — failure costs arise from defects that have occurred, not from checking for them.","Incorrect — prevention costs are spent to stop defects happening, not to find them.","Incorrect — rework corrects defects already found; it is an internal failure cost."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-784",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] is the activity required to correct defects.`,options:["Appraisal","Inspection","Prevention","Rework"],answer:3,hint:"Book glossary p. 588.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 588) defines **rework** as the activity required to correct defects."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The sentence is about correcting defects, not finding or stopping them."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Appraisal and inspection find defects; prevention stops them occurring."}],final:"Correcting defects = rework → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — appraisal is checking to find defects, not correcting them.","Incorrect — inspection detects defects; it does not correct them.","Incorrect — prevention stops defects from occurring; nothing needs correcting.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-785",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

On the DMAIC roadmap, “Estimate COPQ” is a step of the [?] phase.`,options:["Analyze","Control","Define","Measure"],answer:2,hint:"COPQ helps decide whether the project is worth doing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s roadmap (p. 230) lists **Estimate COPQ** as a Define step; it feeds the business case."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The estimate is needed before the project is approved, which happens in Define."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Measure refines COPQ, Analyze finds causes and Control verifies financial impact."}],final:"Estimate COPQ = Define → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Analyze looks for root causes; the cost estimate is needed before that.","Incorrect — Control verifies the financial impact achieved, not the first estimate.","","Incorrect — Measure refines the estimate (“Have you identified more COPQ?”); it is first made in Define."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-786",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Prevention and appraisal costs are together known as the cost of [?].`,options:["conformance","failure","non-conformance","poor quality"],answer:0,hint:"Good-quality spending vs failure spending.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention and appraisal are the **cost of good quality**, also called the cost of **conformance**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Failure costs are the cost of non-conformance — the COPQ."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Failure, non-conformance and poor quality all describe the failure side."}],final:"P + A = cost of conformance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — failure costs are internal and external failure, not prevention and appraisal.","Incorrect — non-conformance means failure costs, the opposite group.","Incorrect — cost of poor quality is the failure part, not prevention and appraisal."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-787",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT a prevention cost?",options:["Quality planning for a new product","Error-proofing a process step","Inspecting finished goods before shipment","Training new staff on the procedure"],answer:2,hint:"Prevention stops defects; one option only finds them.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention costs stop defects happening: training, planning, error-proofing."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Inspecting finished goods finds defects after they are made — appraisal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three act before any defect exists."}],final:"Inspection = appraisal, not prevention → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — quality planning designs defects out, so it is a prevention cost.","Incorrect — error-proofing stops a mistake from being made, so it is a prevention cost.","","Incorrect — training stops errors before they occur, so it is a prevention cost."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-788",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Under the common convention, which cost is NOT part of COPQ?",options:["Scrapping parts that failed final test","Paying warranty claims on faulty units","Reworking orders rejected by billing","Calibrating gauges used for final test"],answer:3,hint:"COPQ = internal failure + external failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"COPQ (common convention) counts only failure costs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Calibration keeps the checking system reliable — an appraisal cost, outside COPQ."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Scrap and rework are internal failure; warranty is external failure — all COPQ."}],final:"Calibration = appraisal, outside COPQ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — scrap from failed tests is an internal failure cost, part of COPQ.","Incorrect — warranty claims are external failure costs, part of COPQ.","Incorrect — rework is an internal failure cost, part of COPQ.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-789",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about COPQ is NOT correct?",options:["COPQ includes only costs that have their own line in the accounts","COPQ is the cost of not doing the right thing right the first time","COPQ can be estimated in Define to support the business case","COPQ includes costs of defects that reach the customer"],answer:0,hint:"Remember the hidden factory.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"COPQ includes **hidden** costs — corrective work paid through normal salaries — as well as visible ones."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Saying COPQ includes only accounted lines ignores the hidden factory, so it is the false statement."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three match the book definition, its Define use and external failure."}],final:"False statement: “only costs with their own accounting line” → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this is the book’s definition of COPQ (p. 584), so the statement is correct.","Incorrect — “Estimate COPQ” is a Define step (p. 230), so the statement is correct.","Incorrect — external failure is part of COPQ, so the statement is correct."],bookRef:"Book pp. 584, 586"},{id:"sv-1-790",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a typical part of a service company’s **hidden factory**?",options:["Manual workarounds for a faulty system screen","SLA service credits shown on the monthly invoice","Reopened incidents that must be worked again","Agents chasing other teams for status updates"],answer:1,hint:"Hidden = unrecognised and uncosted.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The hidden factory is corrective work **nobody recognises** as waste and that has no cost line."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"SLA credits on the invoice are visible external failure costs — above the iceberg’s waterline."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reopened incidents, chasing and workarounds are paid as ordinary work, so they are hidden."}],final:"SLA credits are visible, not hidden → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — manual workarounds are uncosted corrective work: hidden factory.","","Incorrect — reopened incidents are corrective work paid through normal hours: hidden factory.","Incorrect — chasing for updates is unrecognised non-value-added work: hidden factory."],bookRef:"Book p. 586"},{id:"sv-1-791",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a reason for estimating COPQ in the Define phase?",options:["To support the business case for the project","To help the Champion prioritise projects","To prove the root cause of the defects","To show whether the problem is worth solving"],answer:2,hint:"Define justifies and scopes; Analyze proves causes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"COPQ in Define puts a value on the problem for **justification and prioritisation**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A money figure cannot prove why defects occur — root-cause proof is Analyze work."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Worth solving, business case and prioritisation are all genuine reasons."}],final:"Proving root cause is not a COPQ purpose → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — COPQ feeds the business case, so this is a genuine reason.","Incorrect — comparing COPQ helps Champions choose between projects, so this is a genuine reason.","","Incorrect — showing the problem is worth solving is the main reason to estimate COPQ."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-792",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"An assembly plant pays an external laboratory every quarter to **calibrate its torque wrenches** so that bolt-tightening checks give true readings. In which cost-of-quality category is this spending?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:0,hint:"Spending that keeps the checking system accurate belongs with checking.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Appraisal covers the cost of finding defects — inspection, testing, audits **and calibration** of measuring equipment."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Calibrating the wrenches keeps the bolt checks reliable; it supports detection, not correction."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No defect is being repaired, no customer is affected, and the process itself is not being changed."}],final:"Calibration of measuring tools → Appraisal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.","Incorrect — calibration supports checking; prevention would change the process so loose bolts cannot occur."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-793",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"The print house checks a batch of customer bills before posting, finds **misprinted totals** on 2,000 of them and reprints those bills. No misprinted bill reaches a customer. In which category is the reprinting cost?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:2,hint:"Found before or after the customer?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Internal failure = defects found **before** the customer is affected, then corrected or scrapped."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The misprints were caught before posting, so reprinting is internal failure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The pre-posting check itself is appraisal, but the reprint corrects a defect; no customer saw it."}],final:"Caught before posting, then reprinted → Internal failure → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the pre-posting check is appraisal, but reprinting the failed bills is a failure cost.","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","","Incorrect — prevention costs are spent to stop defects from happening in the first place."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-794",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"End-of-line inspection finds a batch of **out-of-specification mouldings**. The moulding line is stopped for three hours while the machine is purged and reset, and the operators stand idle. In which category is the cost of the idle time?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:2,hint:"The defect was found inside the plant.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Internal failure includes scrap, rework and **downtime or overtime caused by defects** found before shipment."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stoppage happens because of a defect found at end-of-line inspection, before any customer is affected."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The inspection is appraisal, but the downtime it triggers is a consequence of the failure."}],final:"Downtime caused by an internally found defect → Internal failure → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the inspection is appraisal, but the idle time caused by the defect is a failure cost.","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","","Incorrect — the purge reacts to a defect already made; prevention would stop the defect being made."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-795",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After three outages in one month, **400 business customers cancel** their contracts and move to a competitor. In which category is the lost revenue?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:1,hint:"The customer was affected.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"External failure includes the consequences of defects that **reach the customer**, including lost customers (churn)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The outages affected customers, who then left, so the lost revenue is external failure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing was found internally, checked or prevented."}],final:"Churn after customer-facing failures → External failure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — appraisal costs are spent checking or inspecting to find defects.","","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.","Incorrect — prevention costs are spent to stop defects from happening in the first place."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-796",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Before launching a new mobile tariff, the team runs a **design review and an FMEA** on the billing set-up to find what could go wrong and builds checks into the configuration. In which category is the cost of this work?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:3,hint:"Is the work done before any defect exists?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention costs are spent to **stop defects happening**: design reviews, FMEA on new designs, error-proofing."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The review happens before launch to design failures out of the tariff set-up."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing is inspected after the fact, repaired or experienced by a customer."}],final:"Design review / FMEA before launch → Prevention → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — appraisal checks finished work; this review designs failures out before anything is produced.","Incorrect — external failure costs arise from defects found by, or affecting, the customer.","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-797",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A fulfilment manager has COPQ figures for 12 failure types and wants to show the sponsor which types cost the **most money**, so the team can attack them first. Which tool is BEST?",options:["An individuals control chart of monthly COPQ","A histogram of the cost of each failure","A scatter diagram of cost against month","A Pareto chart of COPQ by failure type"],answer:3,hint:"Rank categories by a measure of impact.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **Pareto chart** ranks categories from largest to smallest; using cost instead of counts makes it a cost-weighted Pareto."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The manager wants to rank 12 categories by money, so a Pareto of COPQ by failure type fits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A control chart shows stability over time, a histogram shows the shape of numeric data, and a scatter diagram shows a relationship between two variables."}],final:"Ranking categories by cost = Pareto chart → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a control chart shows whether monthly COPQ is stable, not which failure type costs most.","Incorrect — a histogram shows the distribution of a numeric variable, not a ranking of categories.","Incorrect — a scatter diagram looks for a relationship between two variables, not a ranking.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-798",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An operator misses the regulator’s quarterly **service-quality targets** for fault repair and pays a regulatory penalty. In which category is the penalty?",options:["Appraisal","External failure","Internal failure","Prevention"],answer:1,hint:"The failure affected customers and was reported outside the company.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"External failure costs include penalties, credits and fines that follow defects affecting customers."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Late fault repairs affected customers and triggered the penalty, so it is external failure."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The penalty is not spent on checking or on stopping defects, and the failures were not caught internally."}],final:"Penalty for customer-facing failures → External failure → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — appraisal costs are spent checking or inspecting to find defects.","","Incorrect — internal failure costs arise from defects found before the customer is affected.","Incorrect — prevention costs are spent to stop defects from happening in the first place."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-799",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"For her business case, a Black Belt adds up prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure costs and reports the total as the project’s **COPQ**. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because COPQ is the total of all quality-related costs","Yes, because appraisal and prevention are caused by poor quality","No, because the total of all four categories is the cost of quality, not COPQ","No, because COPQ should include external failure costs only"],answer:2,hint:"COQ = P + A + IF + EF; COPQ = IF + EF.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common convention: **COPQ = internal + external failure**; adding prevention and appraisal gives total **COQ**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Reporting the four-category total as COPQ overstates the problem’s cost."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"COPQ includes both failure categories, not only external failure."}],final:"Not appropriate — that total is COQ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the total of all four categories is COQ; COPQ is only the failure part.","Incorrect — prevention and appraisal are the cost of good quality, not of poor quality.","","Incorrect — internal failures such as rework and scrap are also part of COPQ."],trap:"All four categories added together = COQ, not COPQ.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-800",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"To show a quick COPQ reduction, a manager cuts the **training budget** for new provisioning staff by half. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because training is a failure cost and COPQ falls at once","Yes, because training is an appraisal cost that adds no value","No, because training is a prevention cost and cutting it tends to raise failure costs","No, because training costs can only be changed by Finance"],answer:2,hint:"Classify training first, then think of its effect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Training is a **prevention** cost — it is not part of COPQ at all."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cutting prevention usually leads to more errors, so internal and external failure costs rise and total COQ rises."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Training is not appraisal, and the issue is the effect on quality, not who signs the budget."}],final:"Not appropriate — cutting prevention raises failure costs → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — training is prevention, not failure, so cutting it does not lower COPQ.","Incorrect — training is prevention, not appraisal, and it reduces future defects.","","Incorrect — the problem is the quality effect of the cut, not budget authority."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-801",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A COPQ estimate covers rework and SLA credits, but the Champion believes repeated faults are also causing customers to leave. Churn cost is real but hard to measure. What is the BEST action?",options:["Estimate the churn cost, state the assumptions and agree the method with Finance","Leave churn out, because only costs with their own accounting line are COPQ","Add a large round figure for churn to make the business case stronger","Wait until Control to decide whether churn should be counted"],answer:0,hint:"Hard-to-measure costs are estimated openly, not ignored or inflated.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Some costs — churn, lost referrals, reputation — are real but hard to measure."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The book’s advice: **estimate them, state your assumptions and agree the method with Finance**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ignoring them understates COPQ; inventing a figure destroys credibility; waiting until Control is too late for the business case."}],final:"Estimate openly with stated assumptions, agreed with Finance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — COPQ includes hidden costs; leaving churn out understates the problem.","Incorrect — an unsupported number undermines the business case and the Black Belt’s credibility.","Incorrect — the estimate is needed in Define for the business case, not at the end of the project."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-802",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:1,stem:"A Champion asks whether reducing repeat truck rolls is **worth a Black Belt project**. What should the Black Belt do FIRST?",options:["Run a Gage R&R study on the job-closure codes","Estimate the COPQ of the repeat truck rolls","Run a designed experiment on the installation steps","Write a control plan for the dispatch process"],answer:1,hint:"Is it worth solving? Put a money value on it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"“Estimate COPQ” is a **Define** step that shows whether a problem is worth solving."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The Champion’s question is about worth, so the first action is a COPQ estimate."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A DOE belongs in Improve, a control plan in Control and a Gage R&R in Measure."}],final:"Worth doing? → estimate COPQ first → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Gage R&R checks a measurement system in Measure; it does not show whether the project is worth doing.","","Incorrect — a designed experiment is an Improve tool, long after the project is approved.","Incorrect — a control plan is written in Control to sustain gains."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-803",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A customer finds a faulty firmware setting on a delivered gateway. The engineer collects the unit and corrects it in the operator’s own repair workshop. The Black Belt records the cost as **internal failure** because the repair was done in-house. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the repair was done inside the company","Yes, because firmware errors are always internal failures","No, because the customer found the defect, so the cost is external failure","No, because the repair is an appraisal cost"],answer:2,hint:"Category is decided by who found the defect or whom it affected.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The internal/external test is **who found it, or whom it affected** — not where it was fixed."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The customer found the fault, so the collection and repair are external failure, even though the work was in-house."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Firmware errors can be internal or external depending on when they are found, and repairing is not checking."}],final:"Not appropriate — customer-found defect = external failure → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — repair location does not decide the category; who found the defect does.","Incorrect — the type of defect does not decide the category; when it was found does.","","Incorrect — appraisal is checking to find defects; this is repair of a defect the customer found."],trap:"“Fixed in-house” is a distractor — the customer found it.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-804",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt groups one year of quality-related costs:

\`\`\`
Tabulated Statistics: Cost group; Sum of Cost (OMR)

Cost group                         Sum
Conformance (prevention+appraisal)   48,000
Non-conformance (failure)           212,000
All                                 260,000
\`\`\`

Under the common convention, what is the COPQ for the year?`,options:["OMR 260,000","OMR 164,000","OMR 212,000","OMR 48,000"],answer:2,hint:"COPQ = cost of non-conformance (failure costs).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common convention: COPQ = failure costs = the cost of **non-conformance**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Read the Non-conformance row of the output: OMR 212,000."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Conformance row is the cost of good quality; the All row is total COQ; OMR 164,000 is the gap between the two groups, which has no meaning."}],final:"COPQ = non-conformance row = OMR 212,000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 260,000 is the All row, the total cost of quality, not COPQ.","Incorrect — this is the difference between the two groups, which is not a recognised quality-cost total.","","Incorrect — 48,000 is the conformance group (prevention + appraisal), the cost of good quality."],trap:"The “All” row is COQ — do not report it as COPQ.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-805",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A contact centre’s cost-of-quality profile:

\`\`\`
Cost of Quality profile (% of total COQ)

Category           % of COQ
Prevention              4
Appraisal              11
Internal failure       35
External failure       50
\`\`\`

What does this profile suggest?`,options:["The centre spends too much on prevention and should cut training","Appraisal is the main problem and inspection should be removed","The profile is ideal because most costs are recorded","Spending is heavily on failure, so more effort should move to prevention"],answer:3,hint:"Mature quality systems spend more upstream and less on failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A healthy profile shifts spending **upstream**; a high failure share signals too little prevention."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The output shows failure categories at 35% and 50% while prevention is only 4%."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Prevention is the smallest share, removing appraisal would push more defects to customers, and a large failure share is not ideal."}],final:"High failure share → invest in prevention → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — prevention is the smallest share (4%); cutting training would raise failures further.","Incorrect — appraisal is only 11%; removing it would let more defects reach customers.","Incorrect — recording costs is good, but a profile dominated by failure shows poor quality.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.2.3"},{id:"sv-1-806",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team tallies one quarter of installation failures with their total COPQ:

\`\`\`
Tally and Sum: Failure type

Failure type      Count   Sum of COPQ (OMR)
No-show slot        420          8,400
Wrong equipment     150         22,500
Failed splice        90         31,500
Missing permit       60          3,000
\`\`\`

The goal is to reduce COPQ. Which failure type should the team prioritise?`,options:["Failed splice","No-show slot","Wrong equipment","Missing permit"],answer:0,hint:"When the goal is money, rank by cost, not by count.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"When categories differ in cost per event, priority for a COPQ goal comes from the **Sum of COPQ** column."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Failed splice has the largest COPQ (31,500) even though it is one of the less frequent types."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No-show is most frequent but cheapest per event; wrong equipment and missing permit have lower totals."}],final:"Largest Sum of COPQ = Failed splice → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — no-show has the highest count, but its COPQ total is lower; count is the wrong ranking for a cost goal.","Incorrect — wrong equipment is second on COPQ (22,500), behind failed splice.","Incorrect — missing permit has both the lowest count and the lowest COPQ."],trap:"Most frequent is not the same as most costly.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-807",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Training engineers to follow a new installation procedure is a **prevention** cost. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Prevention = spending so that defects do not occur.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prevention costs include training, quality planning, error-proofing and design reviews."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Training is done before any defect exists, to stop installation errors — a prevention cost."}],final:"Training = prevention → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — training is the classic example of a prevention cost."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-808",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Under the common convention, COPQ includes appraisal costs such as final inspection. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"COPQ (common convention) = internal + external failure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Common convention: COPQ = failure costs only; prevention and appraisal are the cost of good quality."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Final inspection is appraisal, so it is outside COPQ (some authors include it — state your convention)."}],final:"Appraisal is outside COPQ (common convention) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — only some authors include appraisal; the exam convention counts failure costs only.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-809",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"Revenue lost from customers who leave after repeated faults is part of COPQ, even though it is hard to measure. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"COPQ includes real but hard-to-measure costs below the iceberg’s waterline.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Lost customers are an external failure consequence; COPQ includes such costs even without an accounting line."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Churn after faults is real COPQ — estimate it, state assumptions and agree with Finance."}],final:"Churn is external failure COPQ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — being hard to measure does not remove a cost from COPQ; it is estimated with stated assumptions."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-810",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put the four cost-of-quality categories in order along the **life of a defect**, from earliest to latest:

- **P** Internal failure
- **Q** Prevention
- **R** External failure
- **S** Appraisal`,options:["Q → S → P → R","S → Q → P → R","Q → P → S → R","P → S → Q → R"],answer:0,hint:"Prevent it → detect it → fix it before the customer → suffer it after the customer.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The life of a defect: **prevent** it, **detect** it by checking, **fix** it before the customer sees it, or **suffer** it after the customer sees it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Prevention (Q) → Appraisal (S) → Internal failure (P) → External failure (R)."}],final:"Q → S → P → R → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — checking cannot come before prevention; prevention acts before the defect exists.","Incorrect — internal failure follows detection by appraisal; a defect must be found before it is fixed.","Incorrect — failure cannot come first; prevention and appraisal act earlier."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-811",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt estimates the yearly COPQ of one failure for the business case. Put the steps in order:

- **P** Find the average cost of one occurrence
- **Q** Agree the estimate and assumptions with Finance
- **R** Define the failure clearly
- **S** Count how often the failure occurs in a period`,options:["Q → R → S → P","R → S → P → Q","S → R → P → Q","R → S → Q → P"],answer:1,hint:"Define → count → cost → agree.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"You cannot count a failure you have not defined, and Finance agrees a finished estimate."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Define the failure (R) → count occurrences (S) → cost per occurrence (P) → agree with Finance (Q)."}],final:"R → S → P → Q → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Finance cannot agree an estimate before the failure is defined, counted and costed.","","Incorrect — counting before the failure is defined gives inconsistent counts.","Incorrect — Finance should agree the completed estimate, after the cost per occurrence is known."],bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-812",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put these COPQ activities in DMAIC order:

- **P** Verify the financial impact with Finance
- **Q** Estimate COPQ for the business case
- **R** Implement the solution
- **S** Refine the COPQ estimate as more data are collected`,options:["Q → R → S → P","S → Q → R → P","Q → S → R → P","Q → S → P → R"],answer:2,hint:"Define estimates, Measure refines, Improve implements, Control verifies.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"“Estimate COPQ” is a Define step; Measure asks “Have you identified more COPQ?”; “Verify Financial Impact” is the last Control step."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Estimate (Q, Define) → refine (S, Measure) → implement (R, Improve) → verify (P, Control)."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — refining COPQ is a Measure activity and comes before implementation.","Incorrect — the estimate is first made in Define, before it can be refined.","","Incorrect — financial impact can only be verified after the solution is implemented."],bookRef:"Book pp. 230–231, 499"},{id:"sv-1-813",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Who first proposed the principle that **most of the influence on an outcome comes from a minority of input factors**?",options:["Walter Shewhart","Vilfredo Pareto","Joseph Juran","W. Edwards Deming"],answer:1,hint:"The principle carries the name of the person who first proposed it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 587) credits the Pareto principle to **Vilfredo Pareto (1848–1923)**, who studied the distribution of wealth in Italy."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stem asks who first proposed the idea, not who applied it to quality."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Juran later applied it to quality; Deming and Shewhart are known for other contributions."}],final:"Principle first proposed by Vilfredo Pareto → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Shewhart is known for the control chart, not the Pareto principle.","","Incorrect — Juran applied the idea to quality problems later and named the “vital few”; he did not first propose it.","Incorrect — Deming is known for the PDSA cycle and his management points, not the Pareto principle."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-814",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which question does a Pareto chart answer?",options:["Is the process stable over time?","How is a measured variable distributed?","Which categories contribute most to the problem?","Why does the problem occur?"],answer:2,hint:"Pareto shows where to look, not why.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart ranks categories so the team can see **which few contribute most** — where to focus."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The chart sorts categories by size; it does not explain causes, time behaviour or shape of a distribution."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Root causes need Analyze tools, stability needs a control chart and distribution shape needs a histogram."}],final:"Pareto = which categories matter most → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — stability over time is judged with a control chart.","Incorrect — the distribution of a measured variable is shown with a histogram.","","Incorrect — a Pareto chart shows where problems cluster, not why; root causes need Analyze tools."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-815",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why are many small categories combined into a single **“Other”** bar on a Pareto chart?",options:["To make the “Other” bar the tallest so it is investigated first","To raise the cumulative percentage of the first bar","To turn the chart into a histogram","To keep the chart readable and the focus on the vital few"],answer:3,hint:"The trivial many are grouped and placed last.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Grouping the trivial many into **“Other”** keeps the chart readable; “Other” is placed **last**, even if it is larger than a small named bar."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The aim is focus on the named vital few, not to promote “Other”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Combining does not change the first bar’s percentage and does not change the type of chart."}],final:"“Other” = trivial many grouped for readability, placed last → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — “Other” mixes many causes and is placed at the far right; it is not a single target for action.","Incorrect — the first bar’s percentage depends only on its own count and the total, which grouping does not change.","Incorrect — a histogram shows numeric data; grouping categories still gives a Pareto chart.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-816",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which tool is designed to separate the **vital few** problem categories from the trivial many?",options:["Pareto chart","Histogram","Affinity diagram","Control chart"],answer:0,hint:"Ranking categories by size = the vital-few tool.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **Pareto chart** sorts categories from largest to smallest with a cumulative line, exposing the vital few."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The stem asks for the tool that ranks problem categories by size."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A histogram shows numeric distributions, an affinity diagram groups ideas by theme, and a control chart tracks stability."}],final:"Vital few = Pareto chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a histogram shows the shape of measurement data, not a ranking of categories.","Incorrect — an affinity diagram groups ideas or VOC comments by theme; it does not rank by size.","Incorrect — a control chart separates common from special-cause variation over time."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-817",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"On a Pareto chart, what does the **height of each bar** show?",options:["The number of root causes found for that category","The count (or impact) for that category, read on the left axis","The cumulative percentage up to that category","The specification limit for that category"],answer:1,hint:"Bars on the left axis; line on the right axis.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart has two vertical axes: **bar heights = counts on the left**, cumulative % line on the right."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So a bar’s height is that category’s own count, or its impact in a weighted Pareto."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Cumulative % is the line, specification limits do not appear, and the chart does not count root causes."}],final:"Bar height = category count (left axis) → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a Pareto chart counts occurrences per category; it does not identify root causes.","","Incorrect — cumulative percentage is shown by the line, read on the right axis.","Incorrect — specification limits belong to histograms and capability studies, not Pareto charts."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-818",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about a **Pareto chart** and a **histogram** is CORRECT?",options:["Both charts show measurement data grouped into intervals","Both charts must show categories in alphabetical order","A histogram shows measurement data; a Pareto chart shows categories in descending order","A histogram shows categories in descending order; a Pareto chart shows measurement data"],answer:2,hint:"Numeric data → histogram; categorical data ranked → Pareto.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary: a **histogram** displays numerical (measurement) data; a **Pareto chart** displays attribute data in descending order."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only one statement keeps each chart with its own data type: histogram = measurements, Pareto = ranked categories."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Swapping the descriptions, treating both as interval charts or alphabetical ordering are all wrong."}],final:"Histogram = numeric; Pareto = ranked categories → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — only a histogram groups measurement data into intervals; a Pareto uses categories.","Incorrect — a Pareto chart is sorted by size; alphabetical order would make it an ordinary bar chart.","","Incorrect — this swaps the two charts’ data types."],bookRef:"Book pp. 586, 587"},{id:"sv-1-819",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the **Pareto principle** are CORRECT?

1. It is a rule of thumb, so real splits such as 70:30 are common.
2. It states that effects are always split exactly 80:20.
3. It identifies the verified root cause of each category.
4. It states that a minority of input factors produces most of the effect.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Book p. 587: most of the influence comes from a minority of factors.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The principle says a minority of factors exerts most of the influence; 80:20 is a rule of thumb, not a law."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 1 and 4 match. “Always exactly 80:20” (2) is false; Pareto never verifies root cause (3)."}],final:"Statements 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is false: the split is a rule of thumb and varies.","Incorrect — statements 2 and 3 are false: no exact split, and no root-cause proof.","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: a Pareto shows where to look, not why.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-820",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which data set is NOT suitable for a Pareto chart without first grouping it into categories?",options:["Reasons for 300 late deliveries","Types of defects found on 500 painted panels","Causes of 150 machine stoppages","Diameters of 200 machined shafts, in millimetres"],answer:3,hint:"Pareto needs attribute (categorical) data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart displays **attribute** data — counts by category."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Shaft diameters are continuous measurements; they suit a histogram, not a Pareto chart."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Delivery reasons, defect types and stoppage causes are all categories with counts."}],final:"Continuous diameters are not Pareto data → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — delivery reasons are categories with counts, ideal for a Pareto chart.","Incorrect — defect types are attribute data, the classic Pareto input.","Incorrect — stoppage causes are categories, suitable for a Pareto chart.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-821",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A paint-line team has counts of nine defect types (runs, dust, orange peel, thin film …) from last month and must decide **which defect types to work on first**. Which tool is BEST?",options:["Pareto chart","Scatter diagram","Run chart","Histogram"],answer:0,hint:"Counts by category → rank them.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Prioritising categories by their counts is the job of a **Pareto chart**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Nine defect types with counts are attribute data; ranking them shows the vital few."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A scatter diagram relates two variables, a run chart shows time order and a histogram shows numeric data."}],final:"Prioritise categories = Pareto chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a scatter diagram examines a relationship between two numeric variables.","Incorrect — a run chart shows data in time order, not a ranking of defect types.","Incorrect — a histogram shows the distribution of measurement data, not counts by defect type."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-822",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt draws a Pareto chart of complaint reasons. **“Billing error”** is the tallest bar, so she reports billing errors as the **verified root cause** of customer complaints. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the tallest bar on a Pareto chart is the root cause","Yes, because Pareto charts are used to verify root causes in Analyze","No, because a Pareto chart shows where complaints cluster, not why they occur","No, because “Billing error” should be placed last on the chart"],answer:2,hint:"Pareto prioritises; it does not prove causes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart shows **where** problems cluster — which category is biggest — not **why** they happen."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“Billing error” is a symptom category; its causes still need Analyze tools and data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The tallest bar is a focus area, not a verified cause, and only “Other” goes last."}],final:"Not appropriate — Pareto shows where, not why → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the tallest bar is the biggest category, a place to focus, not a proven cause.","Incorrect — root causes are verified with Analyze tools and data, not with a Pareto chart alone.","","Incorrect — only “Other” is placed last; the tallest named category goes first."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-823",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"In Define, a Pareto chart shows that two of eight complaint categories account for **75%** of complaints. What is the BEST next action?",options:["Remove the “Other” bar and redraw the chart until it shows 80:20","Focus the problem statement and scope on those two categories","Work on all eight categories equally so nothing is missed","Start with the smallest categories because they are quicker to fix"],answer:1,hint:"The vital few set the focus.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pareto analysis exists to **focus effort on the vital few**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Two categories cause most complaints, so the problem statement and scope should centre on them."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Spreading effort evenly or starting small wastes effort, and forcing an 80:20 split distorts the data."}],final:"Focus scope on the vital few → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 80:20 is only a rule of thumb; manipulating the chart to show it is wrong.","","Incorrect — working on all categories equally ignores the purpose of Pareto analysis.","Incorrect — small categories are the trivial many; fixing them gives little gain."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-824",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A welding cell charts **400** weld defects:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Weld Defect

Defect    Porosity  Undercut  Spatter  Crack  Other
Count          184       124       36     24     32
Percent       46.0      31.0      9.0    6.0    8.0
Cum %         46.0      77.0     86.0   92.0  100.0
\`\`\`

Which is the smallest set of named categories whose cumulative percentage reaches **at least 80%**?`,options:["Porosity only","Porosity, Undercut, Spatter and Crack","Porosity, Undercut and Spatter","Porosity and Undercut"],answer:2,hint:"Read the Cum % row, left to right, until it first reaches 80.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Cum %** row is a running total; read it until it first reaches the threshold."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"After Undercut it is 77.0% (below 80); after Spatter it is 86.0% — the first value at or above 80."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Two categories stop at 77.0%, one at 46.0%; adding Crack goes past the smallest set."}],final:"First Cum % ≥ 80 is 86.0 at Spatter → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Porosity alone is 46.0%, well below 80%.","Incorrect — 80% is already reached at Spatter; Crack is not needed for the smallest set.","","Incorrect — Porosity and Undercut reach only 77.0%, below 80%."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-825",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A service desk charts **800** tickets:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Ticket Category

Category  Access  Network  Email  Hardware  Printing  Other
Count        312      224    104        72        48     40
Percent     39.0     28.0   13.0       9.0       6.0    5.0
Cum %       39.0     67.0   80.0      89.0      95.0  100.0
\`\`\`

What percentage of tickets is due to **Network** alone?`,options:["67.0%","39.0%","13.0%","28.0%"],answer:3,hint:"Percent row = one category; Cum % row = running total.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Percent** row gives each category’s own share; the **Cum %** row adds earlier categories."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"In the Network column, Percent = 28.0."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"67.0 is the Cum % (Access + Network), 39.0 is Access, 13.0 is Email."}],final:"Network alone = Percent row = 28.0% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 67.0% is the cumulative share of Access and Network together.","Incorrect — 39.0% is the share of Access, the first category.","Incorrect — 13.0% is the share of Email, the third category.",""],trap:"Read the Percent row for one category, the Cum % row for a running total.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-826",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put these steps of a Pareto-driven improvement cycle in order:

- **P** Re-run the Pareto chart after the improvement
- **Q** Build a Pareto chart of the current defects
- **R** Choose the new top category for the next cycle
- **S** Improve the top category`,options:["Q → S → R → P","S → Q → P → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → S → P → R"],answer:3,hint:"Chart → fix → re-chart → next target.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"After an improvement the Pareto must be **re-run**; a new top bar often appears and becomes the next focus."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Build Pareto (Q) → improve top category (S) → re-run Pareto (P) → choose new top category (R)."}],final:"Q → S → P → R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the new top category is only known after the chart is re-run.","Incorrect — the team must chart the defects before it knows which category to improve.","Incorrect — re-running the chart before any improvement shows nothing new.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-827",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is shown along the **horizontal axis** of a Pareto chart?",options:["Equal-width intervals of a measured variable","The cumulative percentage from 0% to 100%","The categories, ordered from the largest to the smallest","Time periods, in the order the data were collected"],answer:2,hint:"Categories along the bottom; counts left; cumulative % right.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart plots **attribute categories** along the horizontal axis, sorted in descending order."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So the bottom axis lists categories such as defect types, largest first."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Time order belongs to run and control charts, intervals to histograms, and cumulative % is a vertical (right) axis."}],final:"Horizontal axis = categories, largest first → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — equal-width intervals of a measurement describe a histogram.","Incorrect — cumulative percentage is read on the right-hand vertical axis.","","Incorrect — time periods on the horizontal axis describe a run chart or control chart."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-828",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In Minitab’s Pareto chart output, what does the **Percent** row show for each category?",options:["The running total of shares up to and including that category","The share of categories that lie to the left of that bar","The category’s count compared with the largest category","That category’s count as a share of the total count"],answer:3,hint:"Percent = one category; Cum % = running total.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Minitab prints Count, **Percent** and **Cum %** under each bar."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Percent is the category’s own count as a share of the grand total."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The running total is Cum %; shares of categories and comparisons with the top bar are not printed."}],final:"Percent row = category count ÷ total, as a share → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the running total of shares is the Cum % row.","Incorrect — the output does not show the share of categories; it shows shares of the counted items.","Incorrect — each Percent is relative to the grand total, not to the largest bar.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-829",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Bars alone already rank the categories. What does the **cumulative percentage line** add to a Pareto chart?",options:["It shows how much of the total the top categories explain together, helping to locate the cut-off","It shows whether each category is stable or out of control over time","It shows the cause behind the largest category","It shows the specification limit each category must meet"],answer:0,hint:"Read the line to see the combined share of the leading bars.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The cumulative line is a running sum, so at any bar it shows the **combined share** of that bar and all bars before it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That tells the team how much of the problem the first few categories cover — where the vital few end."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Stability needs a control chart, causes need Analyze tools, and specification limits are not part of a Pareto chart."}],final:"Cumulative line = combined share → locate the vital few → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — stability over time is judged with a control chart, not a cumulative line.","Incorrect — a Pareto chart shows where problems cluster, not why they occur.","Incorrect — specification limits belong to capability work, not to Pareto analysis."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-830",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **Cum %** value printed under a bar of a Pareto chart is CORRECT?",options:["It is read on the left axis with the counts","It includes that bar and every bar to its left","It equals that bar’s own percentage","It includes that bar and every bar to its right"],answer:1,hint:"Cumulative = running total from the left.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cum % is a **running total** from the first (largest) bar to the current bar."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"So the value under a bar adds that bar’s Percent to every Percent before it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A single bar’s share is the Percent row; bars to the right are not yet added; Cum % is read on the right axis."}],final:"Cum % = that bar + all bars to its left → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — cumulative percentage is read on the right axis; the left axis shows counts.","","Incorrect — a bar’s own share is the Percent row; only the first bar has equal Percent and Cum %.","Incorrect — the running total adds bars from the left, not from the right."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-831",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the **vital few** in Pareto analysis is CORRECT?",options:["They are always exactly 20% of the categories","They are the categories grouped into “Other”","They are the small number of categories that account for most of the effect","They are the categories with the cheapest and quickest fixes"],answer:2,hint:"Juran’s phrase: the vital few and the trivial many.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **vital few** are the few categories that cause most of the problem; they become the focus."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the statement about a small number of categories causing most of the effect fits."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Ease of fixing is a separate judgement, 20% is only a rule of thumb, and “Other” holds the trivial many."}],final:"Vital few = few categories, most of the effect → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 80:20 is a rule of thumb; the vital few can be any small share of the categories.","Incorrect — “Other” groups the trivial many, the small categories placed last.","","Incorrect — ease of fixing does not define the vital few; size of contribution does."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-832",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about a Pareto chart are CORRECT?

1. It must show at least ten categories to be valid.
2. It verifies the root cause of each category.
3. Its categories should not overlap.
4. It can rank categories by cost as well as by count.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Distinct categories; count or impact; prioritises but never proves.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pareto categories must be mutually exclusive, and a weighted Pareto ranks by cost, hours or customers instead of counts."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 3 and 4 are true. There is no minimum number of categories (1), and a Pareto never verifies root cause (2)."}],final:"Statements 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statements 1 and 2 are both false: no minimum category count, and no root-cause proof.","Incorrect — statement 2 is false: a Pareto shows where problems cluster, not why.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is false: a Pareto chart can have only a few categories."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-833",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The running total of percentages shown by the line on a Pareto chart is called the [?] percentage.`,options:["average","cumulative","relative","weighted"],answer:1,hint:"A running sum of shares.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The line on a Pareto chart shows the **cumulative** percentage — a running total of each category’s share."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The sentence describes a running total, so the word is cumulative."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An average is not a running total; relative share is each bar’s own Percent; weighted refers to ranking by impact."}],final:"Running total of shares = cumulative percentage → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — averaging percentages is a common mistake; the line is a running sum.","","Incorrect — a relative share is one category’s own percentage, shown by the Percent row.","Incorrect — “weighted” describes a Pareto ranked by cost or hours, not the running-total line."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-834",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Pareto analysis helps a team [?] its improvement effort by showing which categories contribute most to the problem.`,options:["duplicate","prioritise","randomise","standardise"],answer:1,hint:"Pareto is a spotlight for focus.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pareto analysis ranks categories so the team can **prioritise** — focus on the vital few."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Showing which categories contribute most is the basis for choosing where to work first."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Duplicating or randomising effort is the opposite of focus; standardising belongs to Control."}],final:"Pareto = prioritise effort → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — duplicating effort wastes resources; Pareto aims to focus them.","","Incorrect — randomising effort ignores the ranking that the chart provides.","Incorrect — standardising work is a Control-phase activity, not the purpose of Pareto analysis."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-835",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a typical use of Pareto analysis in a Lean Six Sigma project?",options:["Prioritising defect categories to focus the project scope","Ranking FMEA failure modes to decide where to act first","Comparing the defect mix before and after an improvement","Monitoring whether the process stays in statistical control over time"],answer:3,hint:"Pareto ranks categories at a point in time; stability over time is another tool’s job.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pareto analysis ranks categories — for scoping, for FMEA priorities and for before/after comparisons."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Watching for special causes over time is the job of a **control chart**, not a Pareto chart."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other three are standard uses of Pareto ranking."}],final:"Monitoring statistical control = control chart, not Pareto → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — focusing scope on the vital few is the main use of Pareto analysis.","Incorrect — the book uses Pareto ranking in FMEA to decide where corrective action starts.","Incorrect — re-running the Pareto after improvement is good practice; a new top bar often appears.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 124, 587"},{id:"sv-1-836",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A bottling plant’s Pareto chart of **600** rejects shows: Underfill **Percent 45.0, Cum % 45.0**; Cap misaligned **Percent 25.0, Cum % 70.0**; Label skew **Percent 15.0, Cum % 85.0**. The supervisor says: “Underfill and cap problems cause **80%** of our rejects.” Which response is CORRECT?",options:["No — the chart shows those two categories cause 70%; Label skew is needed to pass 80%","Yes — the 80:20 rule means the top two categories always give 80%","Yes — the Cum % of 85.0 belongs to the top two categories","No — Underfill alone already causes more than 80%"],answer:0,hint:"Read the Cum % under the second bar.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Cum %** under a bar gives the combined share of that bar and all bars before it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Under Cap misaligned the Cum % is 70.0, so the top two explain 70%; 85.0 appears only after Label skew."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"80:20 is not guaranteed, 85.0 belongs to three categories, and Underfill alone is 45.0%."}],final:"Top two = 70.0% (Cum % at bar 2) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — 80:20 is a rule of thumb; the chart must be read, and it shows 70%.","Incorrect — 85.0 is the Cum % after the third category, Label skew, not after the second.","Incorrect — Underfill alone is 45.0%, well below 80%."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-837",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt has **500 free-text customer comments** about a new app. She wants a Pareto chart of complaint themes, but the comments are not yet grouped. Which tool should she use FIRST?",options:["Scatter diagram","Affinity diagram","Pareto chart","Control chart"],answer:1,hint:"A Pareto chart needs defined categories with counts.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A Pareto chart needs **attribute categories with counts**; raw comments must first be grouped into themes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"An **affinity diagram** groups ideas or VOC comments into natural themes, which can then be counted and charted."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Drawing the Pareto first is impossible without categories; control and scatter charts need numeric data."}],final:"Group comments into themes first = affinity diagram → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a scatter diagram relates two numeric variables; free text has none.","","Incorrect — a Pareto chart can only be drawn once the comments are grouped into countable categories.","Incorrect — a control chart monitors a measure over time; it cannot group comments."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-838",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"The Pareto principle states that a **minority** of input factors usually accounts for most of the influence on an outcome. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Book glossary p. 587.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 587): most of the influence on an outcome comes from a **minority** of input factors."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement matches the definition; 80:20 is the usual rule-of-thumb expression of it."}],final:"Minority of factors, most of the influence → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this is the book’s own definition of the Pareto principle."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-839",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A service desk tallies tickets: **Email 80**, **Other 120**, **Network 260**, **Access 380** (“Other” combines nine small categories). In what order should the bars appear on the Pareto chart, from left to right?

- **P** Email
- **Q** Other
- **R** Network
- **S** Access`,options:["S → R → Q → P","S → R → P → Q","P → Q → R → S","Q → S → R → P"],answer:1,hint:"Largest named category first; “Other” always last.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Bars are sorted from largest to smallest, but the combined **“Other” bar goes last**, even when it is larger than a named bar."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Access (S) → Network (R) → Email (P) → Other (Q)."}],final:"S → R → P → Q → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — “Other” is placed last even though it is larger than Email.","","Incorrect — this is ascending order; a Pareto chart sorts from largest to smallest.","Incorrect — “Other” is never placed first; it mixes many small categories."],trap:"“Other” goes last whatever its size.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-840",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A rail-signalling team produced this chart:

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Delay Minutes by Cause

Cause     Signal  Points  Power  Track  Other
Minutes     2400    1500    900    600    600
Percent     40.0    25.0   15.0   10.0   10.0
Cum %       40.0    65.0   80.0   90.0  100.0
\`\`\`

Which statement is CORRECT?`,options:["Signal is proven to be the root cause of the delays","Points and Power together cause 80.0% of the delay minutes","Signal causes the most delay minutes, but the chart does not show that it occurs most often","Signal is the most frequent cause of delays"],answer:2,hint:"Check what the bars measure: occurrences or impact?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"This is a **weighted** Pareto: the bars measure **delay minutes**, not the number of incidents."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Signal ranks first on minutes (40.0%); how often it occurs is not shown."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Frequency would need a count Pareto, a Pareto never proves root cause, and 80.0 is the Cum % for Signal, Points and Power together."}],final:"Bars = minutes (impact), not frequency → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a Pareto shows where delay clusters, not why it happens.","Incorrect — 80.0 is the Cum % after Power, which includes Signal as well.","","Incorrect — the chart measures minutes of delay; a cause with few long incidents can top it."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"}];export{e as items};
